 **`focus()` on a window that lives on another desktop switches to that desktop first**, so focus never lands on a window the user cannot see. `HOOKS.DESKTOP_SWITCHED` fires before the window's own `WINDOW_FOCUSED`. `open()` never does this: a window opened onto an inactive desktop (session restore, a lazily loaded native window) joins it without taking focus, and the active desktop stays where it is.
 
+#### Keep on top — Stable
+
+A window **kept on top** stays above every window that isn't, whichever one has focus — including windows opened after it. The user toggles it with the pin at the head of the title bar's controls (`core/keep-on-top`); code does it with the manager:
+
+```javascript
+wp.os.windowManager.setAlwaysOnTop( 'my-plugin-reference', true );
+wp.os.windowManager.getById( 'my-plugin-reference' ).alwaysOnTop; // true
+```
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| **Several at once** | Any number of windows can be kept on top. Among themselves they stack in normal focus order: focusing one brings it to the front of the kept group, never above nothing. |
+| **Focus** | Unchanged. Focusing an ordinary window makes it the focused window (`getFocused()`, `isFocused()`, `os-window-focused`) even though a kept one paints above it. Turning the pin on or off does not move focus either. |
+| **Turning it off** | The window drops back into normal stacking at its place in focus order. |
+| **Child windows** | A child of a kept window is kept with it, so it is never buried under its owner. |
+| **Fullscreen** | A fullscreen window still covers everything, kept windows included. |
+| **Session** | Saved with the window (`alwaysOnTop: true` in the session entry) and restored on reload. Openers can pass `alwaysOnTop: true` in the `open()` config to start a window kept on top. |
+| **Events** | [`os-window-always-on-top-changed`](#os-window-always-on-top-changed--stable) and the `os.window.always-on-top-changed` action, `{ windowId, alwaysOnTop }`. Neither fires when the call left the value unchanged. Unknown ids are a silent no-op. |
+
+The pin's label and pressed state follow the window: "Keep on top" when off, "Stop keeping on top" with `aria-pressed="true"` when on. Its glyph is the `pin` key on `<os-window-button>`, and a desktop theme can replace it through the `WINDOW_CONTROL_KEEP_ON_TOP` slot.
+
 #### Child windows — Stable
 
 A **child window** is a real window — own chrome, drag, resize, minimize, taskbar entry — with one rule layered on top: **its owner can never sit above it.** Clicking the owner hands focus to the child and shakes it rather than raising the owner.
